    "episode_id": 49220,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49220.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "We have made significant accomplishments and although it can feel like they're in the distant past, given how much has been packed into these last 18 months, it's important to reflect on the state of affairs in January 2021 and where we stand now. This president and this Congress took office facing the worst crisis in our democracy since the Civil War, the worst public health crisis since World War I, and the most complicated economic situation since the end of World War II. And 18 months on, We have reopened the economy, gotten Americans back to work at record numbers, reopened the schools, passed really two different series of infrastructure packages, the first of the American rescue plan, the second through the bipartisan infrastructure deal. have tried to recenter American governance on basic competency, integrity, and trust, which was so severely undermined by the previous president. So there has been significant accomplishments, and we would be doing a disservice to not campaign on them, to not remind voters and the press about them, to not be proud of them. But we also, of course, need to recognize that most voters, most Americans right now, are thinking about inflation. The prices are going up, and there needs to be empathy, and there needs to be action associated with inflation.",
