    "turn_text": "talking about inflation, it's important to look at it macro first before digging into the specific dimensions of it. So broadly speaking, from reading and listening across a whole range of expertise and ideology, really, there's consensus that inflation is being driven by three major categories right now. One is a stimulus to aggregate demand. One is supply constraints. And one is the easy money policy of the Fed. And smart people can disagree about the relative weight of each of those three drivers towards the inflation that we're seeing right now. But it's clearly those three things. And I think we need to be candid with the American people about what we can and cannot control and also about the timeline to fixing those three things. So let's go through them one by one here. So the first is the driver of inflation that's based on stimulated aggregate demand. That was based on both Trump era stimulus to the economy during the near of the pandemic, the cares act and follow on relief, which was bipartisan. And then of course, as you said, the American rescue plan, which some economists argued more than covered the output gap of the economy and was inflationary. I think in hindsight, It has contributed to inflation. And yet, it also reduced childhood hunger. It also gave cities and towns 300 billion dollars to make capital improvements to public safety, education, water, transportation. It also lowered healthcare premiums for people in the middle of a pandemic. I think the American people are sophisticated enough for us to say to them, we were facing a crisis. We knew that one of the lessons from the Great Recession in 0809 is that if your stimulus package was too small, you could tip into a prolonged and painful recession. We went big. We went directly to working families with stimulus checks. We went directly to cities and towns. We focused on reopening schools and reopening the economy. We were successful. The economy has come roaring back, but it's overheated. It is. and inflation is one of the byproducts. I think Americans are very much so sophisticated enough to understand that argument. Number two, supply chain constraints. Really, this is about China's zero COVID policy, which has shut down the world's factory, and Russia's invasion of Ukraine, which has set energy prices spiraling. We can do almost nothing to compel Xi Jinping to reopen the Chinese economy when his own political stature is vested in zero COVID. We can do somewhat and have been doing the maximal amount to try to repel Russia's unjust unprovoked invasion of Ukraine. And I've been very vocal on that issue. And reopening markets for energy and grain will help with that issue. But first and foremost, must come Ukraine's sovereignty, prosperity, and democracy. And then finally, we got the easy money policy of the Fed. And the president said in the Wall Street Journal op-ed last week, he respects the independence of the Fed. He's not going to tell them what to do. It's clear that the Fed is set on a hawkish path of 50 basis points or more increases in each of their upcoming meetings. That's the right path, but it's too late. The Fed should have acted sooner.",
