  {
    "episode_id": 49220,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49220.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "You have to. Inflation is, to a large degree, a monetary phenomenon. Despite what these modern monetary theorists might say, you cannot print money and solve economic challenges that way. For 10 years, we had an easy money policy that was very good for Wall Street investors. And there was this consensus that we were stuck in this permanent deflationary period of time based on on an aging population based on economic development in China, based on the commodity cycle. And that's really evaporated over the course of the pandemic. It's clear now that inflation was not fully put to bed under Volcker in the 1980s. And we need the Fed to gain credibility and keep credibility with the markets that it is going to pursue the hawkish policy necessary to get inflation under control. It's really important here to make a distinction between expected and unexpected inflation. What is so painful for long-term investments in this economy, what is so painful for consumers and businesses alike is unexpected inflation in particular. The Fed's credibility is critical in assuring both Main Street and Wall Street that there will not be wild gyrations in inflation going forward. We need the Fed to demonstrate that it is going to do what's necessary to bring down these galloping price hikes. And that is going to mean significant basis point increases in interest rates. That's likely to cool off the housing market. It's likely to cool off some elements of industry. And smarter people than me are saying that this could be a soft landing, reminiscent of the early 90s or the early 2000s. There's no reason, based on what the data we're seeing now, why this has to be like 0809, which was a demand driven recession, much more painful, much more fundamental in that it was coming from the housing market. I don't think that we do not have a situation where things are spiraling out of control. We understand the drivers of inflation and we understand what can counteract it.",
