    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "did, because she was telling me, she said, Jake, the price of the formula is going through the roof. And she was like, I'm a little nervous. And a couple months ago, she bought a lot at one time, which was important because it was very challenging to get in the last few weeks. My mother-in-law actually, a couple of weeks ago, yelled at me for this. She didn't want to know what we're doing about it in Congress. So it definitely hits home. And there's a couple of different drivers of what happened here. There was sort of the near-term, of course, closure of the Abbott facility. But there's also the fact that we had an over concentrated and somewhat brittle market here. We got a couple of key producers who arguably have too much market power and have seized on some regulatory arbitrage to keep out competitors. And then we've got an import regime that makes it too hard to bring in other sources of supply and an understaffed, underfunded FDA who are not able to provide oversight in a streamlined way that allows fast-paced production. So I think there's a couple points of failure here, and it feeds into a much broader narrative that's important to me about what I think of as supply side progressivism, about a progressive vision for the economy that is focused on both sides of the equation, both the supply and demand curves.",
