    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49220.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "to accept. Well, I'm going to vote for anything that passes the Senate. We cannot let perfectly the enemy the good. We need action. And if even a diluted incremental package in the Senate focused on red flag laws and some carrots and sticks for states to do the right thing on background checks, if that even saves one life, if that even prevents one school shooting, it'll of course. be supremely worth it. But make no mistake, I'm just appalled by the obstructionism from the GOP. And you've probably gotten a sense from this conversation and hopefully from the way I've approached the first 18 months, I'm not a rabid ideologue on issues. I like reaching across the island talking to the GOP. I like healthy disagreement. I worked for Charlie Baker, the Republican governor of Massachusetts for a year. But on this issue, I think the GOP has deranged. They are bought and paid for by the NRA. That organization is enabling and indeed inspiring terrorism at the domestic level. And the GRP's infatuation with assault weapons in particular is juvenile and dangerous. And it is a policy decision that is leading to gun slaughter. This is a choice we're making as a country. Since Columbine, 300,000 school children have been directly exposed to gun violence in their schools. That is grotesque. And it's a decision that we make every single day in Congress by not enacting legislation that has the support of a majority of Americans.",
