  {
    "episode_id": 49220,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49220.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "I think there's a couple of different goals here. One is that we've got to record for posterity what happened. This is an underappreciated function of Congress, which is information collection and sense making. Too often, Congress is just viewed as a platform from which people can pontificate, but Congress also has a very important job of just gathering the facts. recording them, analyzing them, and providing a place where there is a high degree of trust in the veracity and interpretation of events. We saw this at the 9-11 Commission. We see this on a variety of esoteric policy areas where Congress just gets the facts, reports it, and it becomes a basis for further work. So very, one, we have to record for posterity what happened. Even for an emotionally charged issue like this, it's important just to get the facts down. Number two is, We need to lay out the case for the fact that the president was directly in violation of the 14th Amendment. The president incited insurrection against the United States government. And I think we need to have that fact pattern be made clear and be made clear in a way that is resonant with the American body politic.",
