  {
    "episode_id": 49220,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49220.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "As you know, we don't have a national election system. We've got 50 state election systems. The critical fights are twofold. One is at the state level. And that to me might even be the most important set of fights. It's who are the registrars? Who are the secretaries of state? Uh, what state laws are being passed? That's why organizations like run for something that are trying to get thousands of local and state election officials elected who will commit to just, you know, being referees, calling balls and strikes. That's why those initiatives are so important. We've got to fight this state by state. Number two is the role of Congress in its ministerial capacity to record and tabulate the Electoral College votes. There, we've got the Electoral Count Act that seems to be bipartisan in the Senate. Seems that people are getting on board with that. That would close the loophole that could have been exploited in 2020 had Vice President Pence not prevented it. That's important too, but we should make no mistake as a country. We should not think that by updating the ECA, we somehow avert the MAGA crowd trying to steal the election in 2024. It'll make it harder for them to steal, but ultimately, if you've got governors and secretaries of state and election clerks doing the wrong thing at the state level, it actually gets very, very hard to fix it at the federal level.",
